/**

@page page_toolbox_loadbalancing_strategies Load balancing strategies

@tableofcontents

# The splitting problem

A run provides @f$ N_{ranks} \cdot N_{trees} @f$ compute units: every MPI rank
hosts up to @f$ N_{trees} @f$ spacetrees (by default one per thread, set with
`-trees`). The spacetree is built level by level, and the only decomposition
operation is a split: an existing tree forks a requested number of its
unrefined cells off into a new tree. Splits cannot be merged back during the
initial decomposition, so every cut is final. The goal is one tree per compute
unit, with the leaves spread as evenly as possible, decided while the grid is
still under construction: early splits let the construction itself run in
parallel and move little data, while later splits see a finer mesh and can cut
more accurately.

The load balancer resolves this trade-off deterministically with two
strategies that run one after the other and converge before the grid
construction finishes.

# Model and objective

Let @f$ \mathcal{T} @f$ be the spacetree, @f$ \mathcal{T}_{leaves} @f$ its
leaves, and @f$ \mathcal{P} @f$ the available compute units. A mapping
@f$ C:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{P} @f$ assigns every node to one unit.
The operation @f$ split(p_i,p_j,n,N) @f$ moves @f$ n @f$ eligible leaves from
a populated source @f$ p_i @f$ to an empty target @f$ p_j @f$ while preserving
the tree topology. @f$ N @f$ is the source tree size when the split runs. The
initial decomposition has no merge operation, so later decisions cannot undo
an early cut.

The primary objective is a small leaf imbalance

@f[
  \max_i |\mathcal{T}_{leaves}(p_i)| -
  \min_i |\mathcal{T}_{leaves}(p_i)|.
@f]

A useful cost model for one split is
@f$ F(split)=A_1 n+A_2 N @f$: both the transferred data and the traversal of
the source tree matter. A combined quality score is

@f[
  w = C_1\,\text{Imbalance} + C_2\sum F(split)
      + C_3\,\text{Surface}.
@f]

For initial construction, balance has the highest priority and the surface
term is small as long as every tree remains one connected space-filling-curve
segment. Splitting early reduces @f$ N @f$ and lets later refinement run in
parallel. Waiting exposes more of the final tree and therefore permits a more
accurate cut. During construction, the balancer has to trade these effects
without knowing the adaptive tree in advance.

# When a cut fires

Before the uniform grid is complete, a cut fires only if every prospective
piece holds at least one coarse-cell-worth of fine cells,
@f$ 3^d @f$ (LoadBalancer::minPieceSizeForInitialSplits()). This gives a
worst-case floor/ceil efficiency of at least @f$ 3^d/(3^d+1) @f$. The first
eligible level depends on the number of pieces created by that cut and the
dimension. Rank spreading can therefore start earlier than a full one-shot
decomposition because each source creates fewer pieces. A 12-piece one-shot
cut first becomes eligible at uniform level 3 in 2D and level 2 in 3D,
independent of the final grid depth.

Once the runner reports that every leaf has reached the configured minimum
level (LoadBalancer::notifyUniformGridComplete()), waiting cannot improve the
uniform mesh. If the configured tree target exceeds the globally reduced leaf
count, the run terminates before another split and reports both counts.
Otherwise the strategies use the completed uniform mesh without the
@f$ 3^d @f$ threshold. This can create smaller pieces: 81 cells over 12 units
give 6 or 7 cells per tree (85.71 %), while a base grid one level deeper gives
60 or 61 cells from 729 (98.36 %). The target is never silently capped.

Every cut requires a quiet grid (LoadBalancer::mayTriggerSplits()): a fork
spans several sweeps, and cutting a grid with in-flight refinement makes the
cell-count request grab cells of the wrong resolution. The runner keeps the
grid static while forks deploy, and a blacklist cools freshly split trees
down until the fork shows up in the statistics. LoadBalancer::update()
completes the preceding global statistics reduction before making a decision
and starts the next reduction after publishing new forks.

# How the domain is cut

- The **initial inter-rank split** (State::InitialInterRankSplit) spreads the
  domain over the MPI ranks. While one rank owns the whole domain, it cuts
  the original tree for every compute unit of every rank in one shot if the
  mesh already supports that; otherwise it deploys one tree per rank as soon
  as those pieces are large enough, and each rank cuts its tree further after
  refinement exposes enough cells. A freshly forked tree refines once before
  its next split because a cell is eligible only when that tree owns its coarse
  parent. All ranks remain in this phase until the global fan-out completes. A
  quiet, partially populated domain with splittable cells violates the
  sole-owner fan-out invariant and terminates the run.
- The **initial intra-rank split** (State::InitialIntraRankSplit) fills the
  remaining slots of a rank: it cuts the heaviest local tree into one piece
  per free slot in one even shot. Before uniform construction ends, a shared
  global load threshold prevents ranks with slightly different local shares
  from cutting at different refinement levels.

Both strategies hand out consecutive sections of the space-filling curve, so
every tree is one unbroken curve segment. State::Stagnation records that the
initial balancer has no valid action. On a completed uniform mesh, it follows
a settled exact target. On an adaptive mesh, hanging vertices can make the
remaining slots unreachable, so stagnation can occur below the target.

# Guarantees

The exact load guarantees assume SplitInstruction::Mode::BottomUp, which is
the mode selected by DefaultConfiguration. A custom configuration using
SplitInstruction::Mode::AggressiveTopDown can exceed a requested cell count.

- The decomposition is deterministic: the same setup produces the same cuts
  in the same order on every run.
- A completed uniform grid that has fewer leaves than the configured target
  aborts as soon as its coherent global view is available. The error reports
  the requested and available counts.
- A direct one-shot decomposition assigns tree and rank loads that differ by
  at most one cell at the cut. When it fires at its piece-size level, the
  efficiency is at least @f$ 3^d/(3^d+1) @f$ (90 % in 2D, 96.4 % in 3D).
  Uniform refinement preserves this ratio.
- A rank-first cascade assigns floor/ceil rank totals in every fork batch.
  Subsequent refinement can amplify a one-cell rank difference, so its final
  global tree efficiency depends on the granularity of the initial rank
  pieces.

# Extensions

The model admits several later refinements:

- A hierarchy-aware cost can distinguish inter-node MPI traffic from
  intra-node tree placement and map the decomposition onto nodes, ranks, and
  cores in stages.
- Weighted tree nodes can represent unequal solver work or a known likelihood
  of further refinement instead of treating every leaf as equally expensive.
- Guided construction can let the balancer request refinement in selected
  regions before committing to another irreversible split.

# Dynamic load balancing

The initial decomposition prioritises fast, deterministic grid construction.
A dynamic balancer can use the established grid and measured workload to
correct imbalance through joins and re-splits. The states
State::InterRankBalancing and State::IntraRankBalancing are placeholders, and
this dynamic rebalancing is not implemented. Adaptive meshes that finish the
initial phase below the configured target require dynamic rebalancing.

*/
